\chapter{Locations}
\label{ch:locations}

A \emph{location} is a place the player can stand in: a room in the labyrinth,
a clearing, a cave.  It holds the entities currently present
(\cref{ch:entities}) and links to the places a player can move to from here ---
the maze chapter will wire those links into a labyrinth and the map chapter
will walk them.

This chapter is the counterpoint to the Entities chapter, and the contrast is
the point.  An [[Entity]] is frozen: authored content that never changes once
written.  A [[Location]] is its opposite on every axis, and for reasons worth
stating before any code:

\begin{description}
  \item[It is wired after construction.] A labyrinth is a graph with cycles ---
    going north from one room reaches a second whose south returns to the
    first.  A value sealed in its constructor cannot tie such a knot, so
    locations are created first and \emph{linked} afterwards; the links must
    therefore be mutable.
  \item[Its contents change.] The Entities chapter deferred the act of
    \emph{taking} an item to \enquote{the world that holds one}
    (\cref{ch:entities}); this chapter is that world.  When the player pockets
    a key it leaves the room, so a location's entities are mutable game state,
    not fixed content.
  \item[It is identified, not compared.] Two rooms with identical descriptions
    are still two different rooms --- a location is the node it \emph{is}, not
    the data it carries.  We therefore compare locations by identity rather
    than by value, which as a bonus keeps them hashable for the discovery sets
    a later chapter needs (\cref{ch:overview}) and sidesteps a non-terminating
    value comparison over the cyclic link graph.
\end{description}

So where [[Entity]] is immutable content, [[Location]] is mutable world: the
structure those contents and links hang on.  The module borrows the package's
language helpers and the engine's [[Message]] event, exactly as the entities
module does, and imports nothing else --- the core still touches no user
interface.

The buckets are the usual ones, with a single twist at the end: the
[[direction registry]] (a table of named directions introduced later) is built
by instantiating a class, so it must be assembled \emph{after} the classes it
uses --- hence its own bucket rather than a slot in [[constants]].

<<[[locations.py]]>>=
"""World locations --- the rooms the player moves between.

A Location holds the entities currently in it and links to neighbouring
locations, possibly in another map.  Unlike an Entity, a Location is mutable:
its links are wired up after construction and its contents change as the player
acts.  This module imports no user interface and calls neither ``print`` nor
``input``.
"""

<<imports>>

<<constants>>

<<functions>>

<<classes>>

<<direction registry>>
@

<<imports>>=
from dataclasses import dataclass, field

from maventyr import DEFAULT_LANGUAGE, localize
from maventyr.game import Message
@

\section{The location}

The fields split cleanly into the three things the chapter opening promised a
location ties together.  The [[id]] is the stable handle, as it is for an
entity --- the maze generator, the saved game and the renderer all refer to a
room by it.  [[name]] and [[description]] are the localizable content shown
when the place is named or looked at.  The last two fields are the mutable
world: [[entities]] is what is currently here, and [[exits]] maps a direction
to the [[Location]] it leads to.

Both mutable fields default to \emph{empty}, built fresh per instance through
[[field(default_factory=...)]] rather than a literal [[[]]] or [[{}]]: a bare
mutable default would be shared by every location ever created, so placing a
key in one cave would silently place it in all of them.  A new room therefore
starts empty and is furnished and linked afterwards.

<<classes>>=
@dataclass(eq=False, repr=False)
class Location:
    """A place the player can stand in, holding entities and exits.

    Mutable, unlike an Entity: links are wired after construction and the
    entities present change as the player acts.  Compared by identity, so two
    rooms with the same content are still distinct rooms.
    """

    id: str
    name: dict
    description: dict
    entities: list = field(default_factory=list)
    exits: dict = field(default_factory=dict)

    <<location methods>>
@

The [[eq=False]] is the third bullet of the opening made mechanical: it tells
the dataclass \emph{not} to synthesise value equality, so a location keeps the
object's own identity-based [[==]] and hash.  This is what we want on a graph
of nodes --- a room equals only itself --- and it is also what makes the
mutable, cyclic structure safe to compare and to drop into a set, neither of
which a value comparison could do without recursing forever.  We turn off the
generated [[repr]] for the same reason and supply our own that names the node
by its [[id]], so printing a room while debugging the maze shows the room, not
an unbounded walk over its neighbours.

<<location methods>>=
def __repr__(self):
    return f"Location({self.id!r})"
@

We verify the content is held, and pin the identity semantics that the rest of
the chapter leans on: distinct rooms are unequal even when their content
matches, a room equals itself, and a room can live in a set.

<<test [[locations.py]]>>=
from maventyr.locations import *
from maventyr.entities import Entity
from maventyr.game import Event, Message


def _place(**overrides):
    fields = dict(
        id="grotta",
        name={"sv": "grottan", "en": "the cave"},
        description={"sv": "En dunkel grotta.", "en": "A dim cave."},
    )
    fields.update(overrides)
    return Location(**fields)


def test_construction_holds_content():
    here = _place()
    assert here.id == "grotta"
    assert localize(here.name, "en") == "the cave"
    assert localize(here.description, "sv") == "En dunkel grotta."


def test_distinct_rooms_are_unequal_even_with_equal_content():
    assert _place() != _place()


def test_a_room_equals_itself_and_is_hashable():
    here = _place()
    assert here == here
    assert here in {here}
@

\section{Linking locations}

Movement is the map chapter's job (\cref{ch:overview}); a location's part is
only to know which way leads where.  We store that as a mapping from a
direction to the [[Location]] reached --- and storing the destination as a
direct object reference, not an index into \enquote{this map}, is what makes a
cross-map link cost nothing extra: a door into another map is the same
assignment as a step to the room next door.  The acceptance's
\enquote{including cross-map links} needs no machinery beyond this.

The primitive is one-way.  [[link]] records that one direction leads somewhere;
it does not assume the way back exists, because a one-way door or a portal into
another map genuinely has no reciprocal.

<<location methods>>=
def link(self, direction, destination):
    """Add a one-way exit ``direction`` leading to ``destination``."""
    self.exits[direction] = destination
@

Most passages in a maze \emph{are} two-way, though, so re-stating both halves
at every carve would be noise.  [[connect]] is the convenience that wires the
pair, taking the return direction as [[opposite]] rather than deriving it: the
geometry of which way is \enquote{back} belongs to the maze generator that
knows the grid, not to a location that knows only its own doorways.  Keeping
that knowledge out of here is why [[connect]] needs no table of opposites.

<<location methods>>=
def connect(self, direction, opposite, destination):
    """Link to ``destination`` both ways, ``opposite`` being the return.

    A convenience over two ``link`` calls for the common two-way passage;
    use ``link`` directly for a one-way door or a cross-map portal.
    """
    self.link(direction, destination)
    destination.link(opposite, self)
@

Looking up a neighbour returns the room or [[None]] when there is no exit that
way.  A missing exit is not an error to raise but a wall to bump into ---
ordinary play --- and the map chapter, not the location, decides how to tell
the player they cannot go there.

<<location methods>>=
def neighbor(self, direction):
    """Return the location reached by ``direction``, or ``None``."""
    return self.exits.get(direction)
@

A one-way [[link]] is visible one way only; [[connect]] makes both halves
resolve; and an unlinked direction is a wall:

<<test [[locations.py]]>>=
def test_link_is_one_way():
    here = _place(id="här")
    there = _place(id="där")
    here.link("norr", there)
    assert here.neighbor("norr") is there
    assert there.neighbor("söder") is None


def test_connect_links_both_ways():
    here = _place(id="här")
    there = _place(id="där")
    here.connect("norr", "söder", there)
    assert here.neighbor("norr") is there
    assert there.neighbor("söder") is here


def test_neighbor_of_a_wall_is_none():
    assert _place().neighbor("norr") is None
@

The cross-map claim is just the reference at work: a location in one map links
to a location in another with the same [[link]], and the neighbour resolves to
that very object.  Here [[cave]] and [[hall]] stand for rooms in two different
maps, joined by a named door:

<<test [[locations.py]]>>=
def test_links_cross_map_by_object_reference():
    cave = _place(id="grotta-i-karta-a")
    hall = _place(id="sal-i-karta-b")
    cave.link("dörr", hall)
    assert cave.neighbor("dörr") is hall
@

\subsection{Which ways can I go?}

A location also has to report the directions it offers --- the renderer and the
[[look]] below both ask.  We return them sorted so the listing is
deterministic; a \emph{meaningful} order (north before east, say) is a
presentation choice that belongs to the renderer, not to the bare set of
doorways.

<<location methods>>=
def directions(self):
    """Return the available exit directions, sorted for stable output."""
    return sorted(self.exits)
@

<<test [[locations.py]]>>=
def test_directions_are_listed_in_sorted_order():
    here = _place()
    here.link("öster", _place(id="ö"))
    here.link("norr", _place(id="n"))
    assert here.directions() == ["norr", "öster"]
@

\section{Holding entities}

The world chapter is where entities finally come and go.  [[add_entity]] is how
the maze-population step (\cref{ch:overview}) drops a character or item into a
room, and [[remove_entity]] is the other end of the [[take]] the Entities
chapter promised but could not finish: taking a key calls this to lift it out
of the room.  We keep [[entities]] a list rather than a set because order is
content --- the sequence things were placed in is the sequence [[look]] reads
them back.

<<location methods>>=
def add_entity(self, entity):
    """Place ``entity`` in this location."""
    self.entities.append(entity)

def remove_entity(self, entity):
    """Remove ``entity`` from this location."""
    self.entities.remove(entity)
@

<<test [[locations.py]]>>=
def test_add_entity_places_it_in_the_location():
    key = Entity(
        id="nyckel",
        name={"sv": "en nyckel"},
        description={"sv": "En rostig nyckel."},
    )
    here = _place()
    here.add_entity(key)
    assert key in here.entities


def test_remove_entity_takes_it_out():
    key = Entity(
        id="nyckel",
        name={"sv": "en nyckel"},
        description={"sv": "En rostig nyckel."},
    )
    here = _place(entities=[key])
    here.remove_entity(key)
    assert key not in here.entities
@

\section{Looking around}

Looking at a location mirrors looking at an entity (\cref{ch:entities}) --- it
returns a list of events for a frontend to render --- but a place has more to
say than a thing: what it looks like, who and what is here, and where you can
go.  We answer with up to three lines.

\subsection{Naming a direction}

Before [[look]] can list the ways out, it must turn an exit's direction token
into words the player reads.  A token is a stable key --- the Swedish
direction, since content is Swedish-first --- but a direction needs \emph{two}
player-facing forms, not one: a noun to name it as an exit (\enquote{norr}) and
an adverb to narrate moving along it (\enquote{norrut}), the latter for the
movement message the map chapter builds (\cref{ch:maps}).  We bundle both forms
with the token in a single [[Direction]] value so the two cannot drift apart,
and so a location's [[look]] and a map's move can ask the \emph{same} object for
whichever form each needs.%
\ltnote{%
  [[Direction]] is a second type, yet we introduce it here --- where [[look]]
  first needs to name an exit --- rather than up front beside [[Location]].  The
  chapter's spine stays on the location; the reader meets directions only once
  the narrative has raised the question they answer.
}

<<classes>>=
@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Direction:
    """A way out of a location, with its localizable player-facing forms.

    Holds the directories for the direction's name (the noun shown when
    listing exits) and its adverb (used when narrating movement); ``adverbs``
    may be ``None`` for a direction that has only a name.
    """

    token: str
    names: dict
    adverbs: dict = None

    def name(self, language=DEFAULT_LANGUAGE):
        """Return the direction's name in ``language`` (e.g. ``"norr"``)."""
        return localize(self.names, language)

    def adverb(self, language=DEFAULT_LANGUAGE):
        """Return the direction's adverb in ``language``, or ``None``."""
        return localize(self.adverbs, language) if self.adverbs else None
@

The four compass points are the directions the maze generator carves
(\cref{ch:maps}), so they seed the registry --- but they are only the seed.  The
table is deliberately \emph{open}: a room may use any token it likes for a
doorway (a named \enquote{dörr}, a portal), and teaching the game a new
direction is just adding an entry here.  English reuses the bare compass word as
its adverb (\enquote{you go north}); Swedish inflects it (\enquote{du går
norrut}), which is exactly why a direction must carry the adverb separately.

<<direction registry>>=
DIRECTIONS = {
    "norr": Direction(
        "norr", {"sv": "norr", "en": "north"}, {"sv": "norrut", "en": "north"}
    ),
    "söder": Direction(
        "söder", {"sv": "söder", "en": "south"}, {"sv": "söderut", "en": "south"}
    ),
    "öster": Direction(
        "öster", {"sv": "öster", "en": "east"}, {"sv": "österut", "en": "east"}
    ),
    "väster": Direction(
        "väster", {"sv": "väster", "en": "west"}, {"sv": "västerut", "en": "west"}
    ),
}
@

Looking a token up returns its registered [[Direction]] or, for a token with no
entry, a fallback that simply names itself and offers no adverb --- so an
unlisted or untranslated exit still renders rather than failing, and the
movement message can fall back to neutral phrasing.  We keep the lookup public
because the renderer resolves directions the same way.

<<functions>>=
def lookup_direction(token):
    """Return the ``Direction`` for ``token``.

    Falls back to a direction that names itself (and has no adverb) when the
    token is not in ``DIRECTIONS``, so any exit token still renders.
    """
    return DIRECTIONS.get(token, Direction(token, {DEFAULT_LANGUAGE: token}))
@

Let us verify that a compass direction carries both forms in both languages,
that an unknown token names itself and has no adverb, and that the name falls
back to the default language like every other string:

<<test [[locations.py]]>>=
def test_cardinal_direction_has_a_name_and_an_adverb():
    norr = lookup_direction("norr")
    assert norr.name("sv") == "norr"
    assert norr.name("en") == "north"
    assert norr.adverb("sv") == "norrut"
    assert norr.adverb("en") == "north"


def test_unknown_direction_names_itself_and_has_no_adverb():
    door = lookup_direction("dörr")
    assert door.name("sv") == "dörr"
    assert door.name("en") == "dörr"
    assert door.adverb("sv") is None


def test_direction_name_falls_back_to_default_language():
    norr = lookup_direction("norr")
    assert norr.name("xx") == norr.name(DEFAULT_LANGUAGE)
@

The description is unconditional --- every place looks like something.  The
other two lines are conditional, and deliberately so: an empty
\enquote{Här finns:} or \enquote{Utgångar:} would read as a bug, so an empty
room is described in silence and only a populated one announces its contents or
its ways out.  The connectors are constant directories like every other
user-facing string, with a [[{...}]] slot for the comma-joined list.

<<constants>>=
HERE = {
    "sv": "Här finns: {items}.",
    "en": "Here is: {items}.",
}
EXITS = {
    "sv": "Utgångar: {dirs}.",
    "en": "Exits: {dirs}.",
}
@

<<location methods>>=
def look(self, language=DEFAULT_LANGUAGE):
    """Return events describing the location, its contents and its exits."""
    events = [Message(localize(self.description, language))]
    if self.entities:
        items = ", ".join(
            localize(entity.name, language) for entity in self.entities
        )
        events.append(Message(localize(HERE, language).format(items=items)))
    if self.exits:
        dirs = ", ".join(
            lookup_direction(token).name(language)
            for token in self.directions()
        )
        events.append(Message(localize(EXITS, language).format(dirs=dirs)))
    return events
@

A furnished room reports all three lines, and reports them in the player's
language --- the entity names localized, the directions localized through
[[DIRECTIONS]], in the sorted order [[directions]] fixed:

<<test [[locations.py]]>>=
def test_look_reports_description_contents_and_exits():
    horse = Entity(
        id="häst",
        name={"sv": "en häst", "en": "a horse"},
        description={"sv": "En lurvig häst.", "en": "A shaggy horse."},
    )
    key = Entity(
        id="nyckel",
        name={"sv": "en nyckel", "en": "a key"},
        description={"sv": "En rostig nyckel.", "en": "A rusty key."},
        takeable=True,
    )
    here = _place(entities=[horse, key])
    here.connect("norr", "söder", _place(id="norr-rum"))
    here.connect("öster", "väster", _place(id="öster-rum"))

    assert here.look("sv") == [
        Message("En dunkel grotta."),
        Message("Här finns: en häst, en nyckel."),
        Message("Utgångar: norr, öster."),
    ]
    assert here.look("en") == [
        Message("A dim cave."),
        Message("Here is: a horse, a key."),
        Message("Exits: north, east."),
    ]
@

An empty dead-end says only what it looks like --- no contents line, no exits
line:

<<test [[locations.py]]>>=
def test_look_of_an_empty_dead_end_is_just_its_description():
    assert _place().look("sv") == [Message("En dunkel grotta.")]
@

A named exit with no [[DIRECTIONS]] entry --- the cross-map door from earlier
--- still renders, falling back to its own token in either language:

<<test [[locations.py]]>>=
def test_named_exit_falls_back_to_its_token():
    here = _place()
    here.link("dörr", _place(id="annan-karta"))
    assert here.look("sv")[-1] == Message("Utgångar: dörr.")
    assert here.look("en")[-1] == Message("Exits: dörr.")
