\section{Resolving a typed direction}

[[lookup_direction]] served \emph{output} --- a token becomes the words shown to
the player.  Movement runs the other way: it starts as a word the player
\emph{typed} (\enquote{norr}, or \enquote{north} when the game runs in English),
and a location keys its exits by token (\cref{ch:maps}).  Without a bridge an
English player would type a word that matches no exit and bump every wall, even
though the map renders perfectly in English --- the display is localized but the
keys are not.  [[resolve_direction]] is that bridge, and the inverse of
[[lookup_direction]]: it turns a typed word back into the token the exit table
and the movement message both expect.

It works by the same open registry, read backwards: a typed word matches a
direction when it equals that direction's token, its name, or its adverb in the
active language, so \enquote{norr}, \enquote{north} and \enquote{norrut} all
resolve to the token [[norr]].  A word that matches nothing is returned
unchanged, which is exactly right for a named exit like \enquote{dörr} or for a
token typed directly: both are already the key their exit is stored under, so
passing them through lets them match as they always did.  Resolution thus never
\emph{blocks} a move --- at worst it leaves the word as it found it.

<<functions>>=
def resolve_direction(word, language=DEFAULT_LANGUAGE):
    """Return the canonical direction token a typed ``word`` names.

    Matches ``word`` against each direction's token, name, or adverb in
    ``language`` (so ``"north"`` and ``"norrut"`` both give ``"norr"``).
    Falls back to the casefolded ``word`` itself when nothing matches, so an
    unregistered or named exit still resolves to a usable token.
    """
    word = word.casefold()
    for token, direction in DIRECTIONS.items():
        forms = {token.casefold(), direction.name(language).casefold()}
        adverb = direction.adverb(language)
        if adverb is not None:
            forms.add(adverb.casefold())
        if word in forms:
            return token
    return word
@

A compass word resolves to its token from either language and whatever its case;
the canonical token resolves to itself even in a language whose word differs;
and an unregistered word passes through unchanged so it can still match an exit
keyed by that very word:

<<test [[locations.py]]>>=
def test_resolves_a_localized_word_to_its_token():
    assert resolve_direction("norr", "sv") == "norr"
    assert resolve_direction("norrut", "sv") == "norr"
    assert resolve_direction("north", "en") == "norr"
    assert resolve_direction("NORTH", "en") == "norr"


def test_canonical_token_resolves_to_itself_in_any_language():
    assert resolve_direction("norr", "en") == "norr"


def test_unregistered_word_resolves_to_itself():
    assert resolve_direction("dörr", "sv") == "dörr"
@

Resolution honours the default-language fallback like every other lookup: with
an unknown language the registry still offers its Swedish forms, so a Swedish
adverb resolves rather than failing:

<<test [[locations.py]]>>=
def test_resolution_falls_back_to_the_default_language():
    assert resolve_direction("norrut", "xx") == "norr"
@
